---
description: APPLY viem best practices WHEN using viem for blockchain interactions. Covers strict typing patterns (Address, Hex, bigint), safe encoding/decoding, ABI typing, error handling, RPC reliability, BigInt formatting, decimal handling, chain-aware clients, and never trusting UI state as on-chain truth.
globs: **/*viem*.ts, **/*viem*.tsx, apps/frontend/**/*.ts, apps/frontend/**/*.tsx
alwaysApply: false
---

Strict Typing:
- Use Address type for all Ethereum addresses
- Use Hex type for hex strings
- Use bigint for all numeric values from chain
- Never use number for chain values
- Avoid implicit type coercions
- Type ABI definitions explicitly
- Use const assertions for ABI arrays

Encoding and Decoding:
- Use encodeFunctionData for function calls
- Use decodeFunctionResult for return values
- Validate encoded data before sending
- Handle encoding errors explicitly
- Use proper ABI types for encoding
- Never manually construct calldata
- Validate hex strings before decoding

ABI Typing:
- Define ABI types explicitly
- Use const as const for ABI definitions
- Type function parameters strictly
- Type return values explicitly
- Use viem type inference when available
- Avoid any in ABI definitions

Error Handling:
- Handle RPC errors explicitly
- Catch and handle network failures
- Validate RPC responses before use
- Use typed error handling
- Never ignore RPC errors
- Log RPC errors with context
- Provide user-friendly error messages

RPC Reliability:
- Handle RPC timeouts explicitly
- Implement retry logic for transient failures
- Use fallback RPC providers when possible
- Validate RPC response structure
- Never assume RPC calls always succeed
- Monitor RPC health and switch if needed

BigInt Formatting:
- Format BigInt values for display only
- Never lose precision when formatting
- Use formatUnits for token amounts
- Use formatEther for ETH amounts
- Keep raw BigInt values for calculations
- Convert to string only for display
- Handle very large BigInt values safely

Decimal Handling:
- Use parseUnits for user input
- Specify decimals explicitly
- Never assume token decimals
- Handle 18 decimals as default but verify
- Use formatUnits with correct decimals
- Validate decimal ranges
- Handle zero decimals explicitly

Chain-Aware Clients:
- Create chain-specific clients when needed
- Validate chain ID before operations
- Use chain-aware RPC endpoints
- Handle chain switching explicitly
- Never assume chain context
- Verify chain compatibility
- Use chain-specific constants

On-Chain Truth:
- Never trust UI state as on-chain truth
- Always read from chain for critical data
- Verify on-chain state after transactions
- Use events to confirm state changes
- Poll chain state when needed
- Never cache critical on-chain data
- Revalidate after every transaction
- Treat indexers and subgraphs as eventually consistent, never authoritative
